==Old English==

===Etymology===
Old English "wyrd" is, derived from Proto-Germanic *wurþiz, Proto-Indo-European *wrti-, a verbal abstract from the root *wert- "to turn" (Latin vertere), related to the Old English verb weorþan, meaning "to grow into, to become" (compare German werden). In its literal sense, it refers to "that which turns out, that which comes to pass". Wyrd is the word, the word of creation - the word said and it becomes reality. German: Wort, scandinavian: ord. The three norns of Fate spoke the word of the becoming future. Sagewomen - those who "say" the word. Swedish: säga (to talk), saga (berättelse). 
The woman who spoke the future were called "Völva", völva from volr - a stick. They spoke their word of fate holding on to a stick, the magic stick which is still the symbol of high spirituel professions like archbishops, magicians like Gandalf and wise old women later demonized as whiches.
Cognate with Old Saxon [[wurd]], Old High German [[wurt]], Old Norse [[urðr]] (Swedish [[öde]]).

